{
  "title": "Metacarpal Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6037/metacarpal-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 22 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Metacarpal Fractures are the most common hand injury and are divided into fractures of the head, neck, or shaft.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made by orthogonal radiographs the hand.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is based on which metacarpal is involved, location of the fracture, and the rotation/angulation of the injury.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "metacarpal fractures account for 40% of all hand injuries",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "men aged 10-29 have highest incidence of metacarpal injuries",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "metacarpal neck is most common site of fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "fifth metacarpal is most commonly injured",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mechanism of injury",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "direct blow to hand or rotational injury with axial load",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "high energy injuries (ie. automobile) may result in multiple fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "wounds may indicate open fractures or concomitant soft tissue injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "tendon laceration",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "neurovascular injury",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "compartment syndrome",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "closed injuries with multiple fractures or dislocations",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "crush injuries",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "concave on palmar surface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "1st, 4th, and 5th digits form mobile borders",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "2nd and 3rd digits form stiffer central pillar",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "index metacarpal is the most firmly fixed, while the thumb metacarpal articulates with the trapezium and acts independently from the others",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "three palmar and four dorsal interossei muscles arise from metacarpal shafts",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Tendons",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**extensor carpi radialis longus/brevis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "insert on the base of metacarpal II, III (respectively); assist with wrist extension and radial flexion of the wrist",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**extensor carpi ulnaris**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inserts on the base of metacarpal V; extends and fixes wrist when digits are being flexed; assists with ulnar flexion of wrist",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**abductor pollicis longus**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inserts on the trapezium and base of metacarpal I; abducts thumb in frontal plane; extends thumb at carpometacarpal joint",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**opponens pollicis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inserts on metacarpal I; flexes metacarpal I to oppose the thumb to the fingertips",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**opponens digiti minimi**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "inserts on the medial surface of metacarpal V; Flexes metacarpal V at carpometacarpal joint when little finger is moved into opposition with tip of thumb; deepens palm of hand.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inspect for open wounds and associated injuries",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "fight wounds over MCP joint are open until proven otherwise",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "extensor tendon can be lacerated and retracted",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "dorsal wounds over metacarpal fractures are almost always open fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "deformity indicates location",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "deformity at metacarpal base may indicate CMC dislocation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "shortening can be assessed by comparing contralateral hand",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "may be prevented by transverse intermetacarpal ligaments in isolated fracture of the 3rd or 4th metatcarpal shafts",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "malrotation assessed by lining up fingernail in partial flexion and full flexion if possible, compare to contralateral side",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6037/images/metacarpal%20rotation.jpg",
                      "caption": "Hand image"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "motor examination",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "typically no motor deficits unless open wounds present",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "check integrity of flexor/extensor tendons in presence of open wounds",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "neurovascular examination",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "dorsal wounds may affect dorsal sensory branch of radial/ulnar nerve",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "volar wounds can involve digital nerves",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
